.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_SESSION_FREE 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_SESSION_new ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_dup ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_up_ref ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_free
.Nd create, free and manage SSL_SESSION structures
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft SSL_SESSION *
.Fo SSL_SESSION_new
.Fa void
.Fc
.Ft SSL_SESSION *
.Fo SSL_SESSION_dup
.Fa "const SSL_SESSION *src"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_SESSION_up_ref
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *ses"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo SSL_SESSION_free
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *session"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_SESSION_new
creates a new SSL_SESSION structure and returns a pointer to it.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_dup
creates a new SSL_SESSION structure that is a copy of
.Fa src .
The copy is not owned by any cache that
.Fa src
may have been in.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_up_ref
increments the reference count on the given SSL_SESSION structure.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_free
decrements the reference count of
.Fa session
and removes the
.Vt SSL_SESSION
structure pointed to by
.Fa session
and frees up the allocated memory, if the reference count has reached 0.
If
.Fa session
is NULL nothing is done.
.Sh NOTES
SSL_SESSION objects are allocated, when a TLS/SSL handshake operation is
successfully completed.
Depending on the settings, see
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_session_cache_mode 3 ,
the SSL_SESSION objects are internally referenced by the SSL_CTX and
linked into its session cache.
SSL objects may be using the SSL_SESSION object; as a session may be
reused, several SSL objects may be using one SSL_SESSION object at the
same time.
It is therefore crucial to keep the reference count (usage information)
correct and not delete an SSL_SESSION object that is still used, as this
may lead to program failures due to dangling pointers.
These failures may also appear delayed, e.g.
when an SSL_SESSION object was completely freed as the reference count
incorrectly became 0, but it is still referenced in the internal session
cache and the cache list is processed during a
.Xr SSL_CTX_flush_sessions 3
operation.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_free
must only be called for SSL_SESSION objects, for which the reference
count was explicitly incremented (e.g.
by calling
.Xr SSL_get1_session 3 ,
see
.Xr SSL_get_session 3 )
or when the SSL_SESSION object was generated outside a TLS handshake
operation, e.g.
by using
.Xr d2i_SSL_SESSION 3 .
It must not be called on other SSL_SESSION objects, as this would cause
incorrect reference counts and therefore program failures.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
SSL_SESSION_new returns a pointer to the newly allocated SSL_SESSION
structure or NULL on error.
.Pp
SSL_SESSION_dup returns a pointer to the new copy or NULL on error.
.Pp
SSL_SESSION_up_ref returns 1 on success or 0 on error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssl 7 ,
.Xr SSL_get_session 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_session_cache_mode 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_flush_sessions 3 ,
.Xr d2i_SSL_SESSION 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn SSL_SESSION_dup
function was added in OpenSSL 1.1.1.
